Z||000000||fropuff||02-18-2001||11:18 AM||||Hey, I've got a 1980 Oldsmobile Cutlass Supreme with the 231 V-6.  It's got a little over 90K on the clock and runs strong.  The thing that I hate is the acceleration, or lack thereof.  This thing gets about 25 for 0-60 flooring it.  Top end is okay; not worried about that.  Sways like a b*tch when I get over 85, so I won't go that fast.  Anyway, I was wondering what engine modifications I can do to it, other than an engine swap.  I don't have too much, a few hundred maybe.  Nitrous is out of the question; I would be going through a bottle a day.  Superchargers and turbos are too expensive, so I don't have much.  What do you think about a rebuild?  Any help is appreciated.  Thanks a lot.||||reg||1
Z||000001||fropuff||02-18-2001||11:33 AM||||One more thing:  I was wondering if there was a way that I could hook up a turbo charger from a mid-80s T-Bird.  I know I said no to turbos, but I found a few cheap ones at yards.  What would I need to fabricate?  Thanks a lot.<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fropuff||02-18-2001||11:54 AM||||Okay, I've been thinking, and I think that I should get a turbo from a larger engine.  I thought the one from the T-Bird would work, but as long as I already have to customize, I might as well get one from another V-6.  I found this one in Canada:<BR>-89 Cougar<BR>-3.8, AT<BR>-Tested good with all tubes<BR>-$450 Canadian<BR>This is in my price range, and it's a good one, so what do you think?  I know I have to modify it to fit, but what would it need?  Thanks again.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cooter||02-18-2001||01:45 PM||||There are factory turbo intakes/parts for the 231 all the time on eBay. I had a complete setup at one time. It used a q-jet carb and a funky looking intake. It sounds like you just have a poor rear end gear ratio.||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnnyK||02-18-2001||01:55 PM||||I agree. Don't know anything about those specific cars, but it sounds like a gearing problem.||||reg||1
Z||000005||LarryB||02-18-2001||02:17 PM||||The 231 (3.8 liter V6) back in those days was a gutless wonder, 1980 was fairly soon after GM bought the rights to the design back from American Motors. It barely made more horsepower than the carbureted 2.8.  (rated 110HP vs 107HP.) The engine was a performance dog until GM added fuel injection. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||fropuff||02-18-2001||02:34 PM||||How would I go about fixing the gearing problem; how much would it be to fix?  I might also go with the turbo; I'm getting all excited.  I found one on eBay for $300 now.  It's at:  <A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/ebayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&Item=557607835&r=0&t=0" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/ebayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&Item=557607835&r=0&t=0</A>   I'm going to email him questions if this will work.  Will it?  Thanks a lot.<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.<p>[This message has been edited by fropuff (edited 02-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||2birds||02-18-2001||07:21 PM||||LarryB said it.  My mom had an 81 Gutless with the same 231 Buick motor.  Not worth messing with.  It's a whole diferent engine from the GNX style Buick 231.  Have you priced any fuel system or emissions parts yet?  Not cheap.<BR>The best (and probably cheapest) thing to do is swap in a Chevrolet small block with fuel injection, and an automatic overdrive.  TPI or TBI, either is an improvement, and probably will be emissions legal, as long as you swap everything over.  <BR>78-87 GM G-bodies (Regal, Cutlass, Monte Carlo, Malibu, Grand Prix) are all pretty much the same. All the handling goodies from a Monte SS will fit.  Find a rear anti-sway bar at a junkyard if you don't already have one.  Big, cheap improvement in handling.  F-body wheels wil fit with no problems, too.  Monte SS's had around a 3.48:1 ratio in the rear ends, but they also had an overdrive, too.  I think that's the best you'll find with an already assembled rear end.  <P>Of course, a 455 Olds would fit nicely, too, but you MIGHT have a bit of trouble at the emissions inspection...||||reg||1
Z||000008||Cooter||02-18-2001||07:40 PM||||BTW- I had an 86 Cutlass with the 231 and a 2 bbl. It was a retired dective car from a local police department and only had 85,000 miles on it. The service records were awesome, everything that could be done to maintain this car was done. And it was still a doggy performer! Mileage was good, comfort was great but it hopeless to get anything out of it. I eventually sold it and got into Fieros. I would have liked to have dropped a TPI 350 and 700r4 in it, but it just cried out as a grand-paw car.||||reg||1
Z||000009||fropuff||02-18-2001||08:24 PM||||That guy already sold the turbo anyway.  No one thinks that a turbo will work?  If I could get a cheap one, I would really like to fabricate it and drop it in.  I was thinking of getting a 350, but I have little cash now.  Maybe $500.  That's why I was thinking of getting a turbo.  If it would work, would I need to get one that works with the carburetor?  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000010||fropuff||02-18-2001||11:33 PM||||Okay, I talked to hugh about this, and he said that I don't just need a turbo, but also a carb to go with.  I found a few and I'm going to the yards tomorrow to see what they've got.  Any other help, suggestions, or recommendations are appreciated.  Thanks a lot.||||reg||1
